// Module
var code = "<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <div id=\"link-to-quiz\" class=\"btn-back\">назад</div>\r\n\r\n  <h1>[St1] Test Algorithms & Data structures</h1>\r\n\r\n  <strong>26 вопросов. Выполнил на 92 баллов из 100</strong>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>1. Despite having a worst-case time complexity of O(n²), which algorithm is often more efficient in practice?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Merge Sort</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Quick Sort</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Bubble Sort</li>\r\n    <li>Binary Search</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>2. In a Binary Search, what is the worst-case time complexity?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>O(n)</li>\r\n    <li><strong>O(log n)</strong></li>\r\n    <li>O(n²)</li>\r\n    <li>O(n log n)</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>3. In a Max Heap, the value of a parent node is __________ the values of its children.</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>less than</li>\r\n    <li><strong>greater than</strong></li>\r\n    <li>equal to</li>\r\n    <li>not related to</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>4. In graph theory, what does 'Dijkstra's Algorithm' compute?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Shortest path in a graph with cycles</li>\r\n    <li>Maximum flow in a network</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Shortest path between two nodes in a weighted graph</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Connectivity in a disjoint graph</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>5. In which usage scenario do Linked List and Array show similar operation execution speed?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Adding an element to the end of the list / array</li>\r\n    <li>Accessing an arbitrary element by index</li>\r\n    <li>Modifying an arbitrary element by index</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Outputting all list / array elements to the console</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Removing an element from the list / array</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>6. In which usage scenarios do Linked List and Array show significantly different operation execution speeds? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Accessing an arbitrary element by index</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Modifying an arbitrary element by index</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Outputting all list / array elements to the console</li>\r\n    <li>Removing an element from the list / array</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>7. What data structure only stores unique elements?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Set</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Map / Object</li>\r\n    <li>Array</li>\r\n    <li>Linked List</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>8. What data structure processes elements in the sequence they were added, adhering to the FIFO (First In First Out) principle?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Stack</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Queue</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Tree</li>\r\n    <li>Linked List</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>9. What does amortized analysis of an algorithm generally provide?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Exact worst-case runtime</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Average case runtime over a sequence of operations</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Comparison with other algorithms</li>\r\n    <li>Memory usage over time</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>10. What does 'hash collision' mean in the context of a Hash Table?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>A function returning the same hash for different keys</strong></li>\r\n    <li>An error when the table is full</li>\r\n    <li>The process of adding a new element</li>\r\n    <li>Deleting an element from the table</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>11. What is primarily described by Big-O Notation?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Memory usage of an algorithm</li>\r\n    <li>Best-case scenario of an algorithm</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Execution time or space used by an algorithm</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Number of operations a CPU can perform</li>\r\n    <li>The size of the headache you get when trying to understand algorithms</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>12. What is the average-case time complexity of Quick Sort?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>O(n)</li>\r\n    <li>O(n²)</li>\r\n    <li><strong>O(n log n)</strong></li>\r\n    <liO(log n)></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>13. What is the best-case time complexity of Merge Sort?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>O(n²)</li>\r\n    <li><strong>O(n log n)</strong></li>\r\n    <li>O(n)</li>\r\n    <li>O(log n)</li>\r\n    <li>O(😅) - when the data is already sorted by magic.</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>14. What is the most common use-case of a Trie data structure?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Sorting</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Fast lookups and insertions</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Balancing other trees</li>\r\n    <li>Network routing</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>15. What is the time complexity of Bubble Sort in its best-case scenario?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>O(n²)</li>\r\n    <li>O(n log n)</li>\r\n    <li><strong>O(n)</strong></li>\r\n    <li>O(log n)</li>\r\n    <li>O(🎈) - as fast as a bubble pops!</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>16. Which algorithm is commonly used for sorting a linked list in O(n log n) time?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Quick Sort</li>\r\n    <li>Bubble Sort</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Merge Sort</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Insertion Sort</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>17. Which Array method checks that all elements satisfy some condition (predicate)?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>.forEach()</li>\r\n    <li>.check()</li>\r\n    <li><strong>.every()</strong></li>\r\n    <li>.some()</li>\r\n    <li>.filter()</li>\r\n    <li>magicMirror()</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>18. Which data structure is most convenient for working with nested properties / parameters?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Set</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Map / Tree / Object</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Array</li>\r\n    <li>Linked List</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>19. Which data structure is typically used for implementing Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) operations?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Queue</li>\r\n    <li>Heap</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Stack</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Linked List</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>20. Which data structure provides fast access by key, which doesn't necessarily have to be numeric or may have missing values in numeric order (e.g., “0”, “4”, “5”, “100”)?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Set</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Map / Object</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Array</li>\r\n    <li>Linked List</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>21. Which data structure provides the fastest access to elements by key, where the key is numeric, starts from 0, and has no missing values in between?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Set</li>\r\n    <li>Map / Object</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Array</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Linked List</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>22. Which elements are accessible in the traditional implementation of the Stack data structure? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Only to the top of the stack (the last added element)</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Only to the base of the stack (the first added element)</li>\r\n    <li>Only to the second element after the top</li>\r\n    <li>Access is available to any element of the stack</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>23. Which graph traversal algorithm employs a queue?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Depth-First Search</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Breadth-First Search</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Binary Search</li>\r\n    <li>Merge Sort</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>24. Which internal (standard) JS structure is the most convenient and fastest to create a Stack?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Set</li>\r\n    <li>Map / Tree / Object</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Array</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Linked List</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>25. Which of the following is not a characteristic of a Max Heap?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Parent node is smaller than children</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Complete binary tree</li>\r\n    <li>Used in Heap Sort</li>\r\n    <li>Parent node is larger than children</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>26. Which sorting algorithm is not stable by default?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Bubble Sort</li>\r\n    <li>Merge Sort</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Quick Sort</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Insertion Sort</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n";
// Exports
/* harmony default export */ __webpack_exports__["default"] = (code);

