var code = "<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <div id=\"link-to-quiz\" class=\"btn-back\">назад</div>\r\n\r\n  <h1>Test [St1] Clean-code</h1>\r\n\r\n  <strong>24 вопроса. Выполнил на 88 баллов из 100</strong>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>1. Complete the statement correctly: \"When naming project files, it is advisable to...\"</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>... start the file name with a capital letter and use the underscore character as a delimiter.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>... use lowercase characters and hyphen as a delimiter.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>... use consistent naming style.</li>\r\n    <li>... use uppercase characters and hyphen as a delimiter.</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>2. Explain a potential drawback of overly strict adherence to DRY principle.</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Increased memory usage</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Decreased code readability</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Slower execution time</li>\r\n    <li>Higher compilation time</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>3. Folding N identical sequences of commands into a loop is an example of which principle?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>SoC</li>\r\n    <li>KISS</li>\r\n    <li><strong>DRY</strong></li>\r\n    <li>SRP</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>4. Grouping repeating CSS rules from different {}-blocks into one is an example of applying which principle?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>KISS</li>\r\n    <li><strong>DRY</strong></li>\r\n    <li>YAGNI</li>\r\n    <li>SMACK</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>5. How can misapplication of the DRY principle lead to software inefficiency?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>By duplicating more code</li>\r\n    <li><strong>By overly abstracting code</strong></li>\r\n    <li>By reducing modularity</li>\r\n    <li>By avoiding code reviews</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>6. How does applying KISS principle benefit new team members?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Reduces onboarding time</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Increases code complexity</li>\r\n    <li>Encourages individual work</li>\r\n    <li>Limits creativity</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>7. How might the YAGNI principle conflict with future-proofing a software application?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>By encouraging excessive refactoring</li>\r\n    <li><strong>By discouraging forward-thinking design</strong></li>\r\n    <li>By overemphasizing user feedback</li>\r\n    <li>By minimizing code documentation</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>8. In what way can the YAGNI principle guide decision-making in Agile methodologies?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>By emphasizing detailed planning</li>\r\n    <li><strong>By prioritizing immediate requirements</strong></li>\r\n    <li>It's great for deciding what to order for lunch</li>\r\n    <li>By encouraging large releases</li>\r\n    <li>By focusing on external APIs</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>9. In which scenario could the DRY principle be most beneficial?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>When writing documentation</li>\r\n    <li>When optimizing performance</li>\r\n    <li><strong>When building reusable functions</strong></li>\r\n    <li>When designing user interfaces</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>10. Putting repeating groups of styles in a separate class is an example of which principle?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Composition over inheritance</li>\r\n    <li>KISS</li>\r\n    <li>YAGNI</li>\r\n    <li><strong>DRY</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>11. What does the DRY principle help to reduce?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Code size</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Execution time</li>\r\n    <li>Redundancies in code</li>\r\n    <li>Compilation errors</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>12. What is accurate regarding the advice on using trailing whitespaces in code?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>The number of spaces is not regulated in any way in the code style guide, as it does not refer to the quality of the code.</li>\r\n    <li>The rule about avoiding trailing whitespaces is a relic of the past. The browser engine is indifferent to their presence in a file.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>By avoiding spaces at the end of a line, you reduce the visible changes in Pull Request, which simplifies the review process.</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>13. What is a risk when ignoring the KISS principle in a project's initial phase?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Faster development cycles</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Increased technical debt</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Faster Software Testing Cycles</li>\r\n    <li>Simplified code architecture</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>14. What is the main focus of the KISS principle in software development?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Complex solutions</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Simple and clear code</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Multiple functionalities</li>\r\n    <li>Long-term planning</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>15. What programming principle advocates against duplicating code solutions?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>KISS</li>\r\n    <li>YAGNI</li>\r\n    <li><strong>DRY</strong></li>\r\n    <li>SOLID</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>16. Which of the following are key benefits of adhering to the KISS principle in software development? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Easier maintenance</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Faster development cycles</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Increased complexity</li>\r\n    <li>Improved scalability</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>17. Which of the following changes can be attributed to an example of following the DRY principle?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Breaking a long code into separate functions.</li>\r\n    <li>Preference of the simplest implementation of the algorithm over too abstruse code.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Separating a combination of commands that occurs several times in the code into a separate function.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Removing an unused module from a project.</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>18. Which of the following options make the following statement true: \"Using the 'left shift' operator by 1 instead of simply multiplying by two:\" (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>... is a more performance efficient operation.</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>... is an example of writing abstruse code.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>... contradicts the YAGNI principle.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>... contradicts the KISS principle.</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>19. Which of the following phrases are variations of the KISS principle? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>keep it simple, silly</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>keep it simple, stupid</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>keep it short and simple</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>keep it simple and straightforward</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>keep it small and simple</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>20. Which of the following statements are arguments in favor of the YAGNI principle? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Writing functionality up front will save time writing it in the future. Take advantage of every opportunity that arises.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Adding new unnecessary functionality can lead to a desire to add even more new unnecessary functionality, leading to a snowball effect.</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Your unnecessary new features may later prevent you from adding new, but already needed, features.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>There is nothing wrong with implementing functionality up front. The main thing is that it should be covered by tests and approved on review.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Superfluous code can also lead to bugs / defects / breakdowns. As a result, you will spend precious time searching for the cause of the breakdown and debugging this code.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>YAGNI encourages developers to add as many features as possible, just in case aliens visit</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>21. Which of the following statements is true?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Leave as many comments as possible. Writing tons of comments will definitely increase your productivity and help other developers understand your code better.</li>\r\n    <li>The complete absence of comments in the project indicates the high quality of the code.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Comments are worth leaving when your decision is not obvious.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Even if your solution seems very obvious, an additional comment will not be superfluous, but on the contrary will increase the readability of the code.</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>22. Which of the following statements is true for the KISS principle?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>It claims that in programming only complex, maximally optimized solutions are correct. The task of a programmer is to be able to deal with code of any level of complexity.</li>\r\n    <li>This principle states that relations between colleagues should be strictly businesslike.</li>\r\n    <li>This principle first began to be applied in 1960, but in the realities of today's developers it is no longer relevant or even toxic.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>A universal principle of any system design. It states that a simple, but not the most optimal solution is preferable to a complex, though maximally efficient in terms of performance.</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>23. Which statement accurately describes TODO comments in programming?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Writing TODO comments is a common practice for forming urgent tasks.</li>\r\n    <li>TODOs, like any other comments, are useless clogging of the project code. Their use is prohibited in large team projects.</li>\r\n    <li>TODO comments are the practice of composing non-urgent tasks in a task tracking system like Jira.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>This type of comment developers leave right in the code to indicate non-urgent but important tasks for the future.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>TODO comments are a secret code for developers to plan weekend parties.</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>24. YAGNI principle discourages what kind of software development practice?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Iterative development</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Adding features not currently needed</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Code refactoring</li>\r\n    <li>Debugging</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n";
// Exports
/* harmony default export */ __webpack_exports__["default"] = (code);

