__webpack_require__.r(__webpack_exports__);
// Module
var code = "<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <div id=\"link-to-quiz\" class=\"btn-back\">назад</div>\r\n\r\n  <h1>[St2] Design Patterns</h1>\r\n\r\n  <strong>33 вопроса. Выполнил на 0 баллов из 100</strong>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>1. Classic MVC standard includes:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>unidirectional data flow</li>\r\n    <li>bidirectional data flow</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>2. Con of Factory Method pattern:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>implements the Open-Closed Principle</li>\r\n    <li>moves the product creation code into one place in the program</li>\r\n    <li>tight coupling between the creator and the concrete products</li>\r\n    <li>can add complexity to the code</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>3. Cons of Proxy pattern: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>complicates the code by introducing new classes</li>\r\n    <li>can work even if the service object is not available</li>\r\n    <li>can't manage the lifecycle of the service object</li>\r\n    <li>the response from the service might get delayed</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>4. Cons of Template Method pattern: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>limitation by the provided skeleton of an algorithm</li>\r\n    <li>making code reuse more difficult</li>\r\n    <li>it's hard to maintain</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>5. In which cases is the Adapter pattern used? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>when you want to use some existing class, but its interface isn't compatible with the rest of your code</li>\r\n    <li>when you want to reuse several existing subclasses that lack some common functionality that can't be added to the superclass</li>\r\n    <li>to simplify the code by introducing a set of new interfaces and classes</li>\r\n    <li>when you want to separate the interface or data conversion code from the primary business logic of the program</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>6. In which cases is the Chain of Responsibility pattern used? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>when your program is expected to process different kinds of requests in various ways, but the exact types of requests and their sequences are unknown beforehand</li>\r\n    <li>when it's essential to execute several handlers in a particular order</li>\r\n    <li>when the set of handlers and their order are not supposed to change at runtime</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>7. In which cases is the Strategy pattern used? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>when you have a lot of similar classes that only differ in the way they execute some behavior</li>\r\n    <li>when your class has a massive conditional statement that switches between different variants of the same algorithm</li>\r\n    <li>when you want to use different variants of an algorithm within an object</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>3. In which cases is the Visitor pattern used? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>when you need to perform an operation on all elements of a complex object structure</li>\r\n    <li>when a behavior makes sense only in some classes of a class hierarchy, but not in others</li>\r\n    <li>when you want to clean up the business logic of auxiliary behaviors</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>15. Is it true that Decorator and Adapter support recursive composition?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>yes</li>\r\n    <li>no</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>1. Is it true that Factory Method is a specialization of Template Method?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>yes</li>\r\n    <li>no</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>13. Is it true that State is a behavioural design pattern?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>yes</li>\r\n    <li>no</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>26. Is it true that the Composite pattern should be used when you need to compose objects into tree structures?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>yes</li>\r\n    <li>no</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>11. Is it true that with Adapter you access an existing object via different interface, and with Decorator you access it via an extended interface?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>yes</li>\r\n    <li>no</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>22. Is it true that you can implement shared leaf nodes of the Composite tree as Flyweights to save some RAM?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>yes</li>\r\n    <li>no</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>10. Iterator is:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>design pattern that lets you separate algorithms from the objects on which they operate</li>\r\n    <li>design pattern that lets you traverse elements of a collection without exposing its underlying representation</li>\r\n    <li>design pattern that lets you save and restore the previous state of an object without revealing the details of its implementation</li>\r\n    <li>design pattern that lets you define a subscription mechanism to notify multiple objects about any events that happen to the object they're observing</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>5. Model in the MVC pattern:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>routes commands to the view and controller</li>\r\n    <li>accepts and handles data from the view</li>\r\n    <li>manages data and business logic</li>\r\n    <li>responsible for displaying the data to the user</li>\r\n    <li>there is no correct answer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>2. Observer is:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>design pattern that lets you define a subscription mechanism to notify multiple objects about any events that happen to the object they're observing</li>\r\n    <li>design pattern that lets you make the primary classes of your app more focused on their main jobs by extracting all other behaviors into a set of visitor classes</li>\r\n    <li>design pattern that lets you save and restore the previous state of an object without revealing the details of its implementation</li>\r\n    <li>design pattern that lets you traverse elements of a collection without exposing its underlying representation</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>29. Pros of Mediator pattern: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>allows to reduce the coupling between components</li>\r\n    <li>simplifies communication between components</li>\r\n    <li>extracts the communications between various components into a single place</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>9. View in the MVC pattern:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>routes commands to the model and controller</li>\r\n    <li>manages data and business logic</li>\r\n    <li>responsible for displaying the data to the user</li>\r\n    <li>contains logic that updates the model</li>\r\n    <li>there is no correct answer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>19. Visitor is:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>behavioral design pattern</li>\r\n    <li>creational design pattern</li>\r\n    <li>structural design pattern</li>\r\n    <li>there is no correct answer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>14. Which design pattern allows objects with incompatible interfaces to collaborate?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Adapter</li>\r\n    <li>Facade</li>\r\n    <li>Decorator</li>\r\n    <li>Strategy</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>7. Which design pattern allows to construct complex objects step by step?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Abstract Factory</li>\r\n    <li>Builder</li>\r\n    <li>Prototype</li>\r\n    <li>Singleton</li>\r\n    <li>Factory Method</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>17. Which design pattern allows you to create objects without showing the logic of instantiation in the client code?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Factory Method</li>\r\n    <li>Abstract Factory</li>\r\n    <li>Builder</li>\r\n    <li>Prototype</li>\r\n    <li>Singleton</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>6. Which design pattern defines the skeleton of an algorithm in the superclass but lets subclasses override specific steps of the algorithm without changing its structure?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>State</li>\r\n    <li>Memento</li>\r\n    <li>Abstract Factory</li>\r\n    <li>Template Method</li>\r\n    <li>Command</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>24. Which design pattern ensures that a class has only one instance and provides a global access point to that instance?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Builder</li>\r\n    <li>Facade</li>\r\n    <li>Adapter</li>\r\n    <li>Singleton</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>32. Which design pattern helps multiple objects stay up-to-date with the latest changes of a subscription object?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Facade</li>\r\n    <li>Strategy</li>\r\n    <li>Observer</li>\r\n    <li>Decorator</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>23. Which design pattern lets you attach new behaviors to objects by placing these objects inside special wrapper objects that contain the behaviors?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Strategy</li>\r\n    <li>Decorator</li>\r\n    <li>Adapter</li>\r\n    <li>Facade</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>4. Which design pattern lets you save and restore the previous state of an object without revealing the details of its implementation?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Command</li>\r\n    <li>Prototype</li>\r\n    <li>Memento</li>\r\n    <li>Mediator</li>\r\n    <li>Observer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>28. Which design pattern provides an interface for creating objects in a superclass, but allows subclasses to alter the type of objects that will be created?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Builder</li>\r\n    <li>Factory Method</li>\r\n    <li>Decorator</li>\r\n    <li>Template Method</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>12. Which design pattern splits a large class or a set of closely related classes into two separate hierarchies — abstraction and implementation?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Proxy</li>\r\n    <li>Flyweight</li>\r\n    <li>Facade</li>\r\n    <li>Decorator</li>\r\n    <li>Composite</li>\r\n    <li>Bridge</li>\r\n    <li>Adapter</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>27. Which of the following are Behavioral Design Patterns? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Singleton</li>\r\n    <li>Adapter</li>\r\n    <li>Facade</li>\r\n    <li>Builder</li>\r\n    <li>Template Method</li>\r\n    <li>Factory Method</li>\r\n    <li>Strategy</li>\r\n    <li>Observer</li>\r\n    <li>Decorator</li>\r\n    <li>Abstract Factory</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>16. Which of the following are Creational Design Patterns? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Singleton</li>\r\n    <li>Adapter</li>\r\n    <li>Facade</li>\r\n    <li>Builder</li>\r\n    <li>Template Method</li>\r\n    <li>Factory Method</li>\r\n    <li>Strategy</li>\r\n    <li>Observer</li>\r\n    <li>Decorator</li>\r\n    <li>Abstract Factory</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n<h3>33. Which of the following is not a design pattern classification? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>Architectural Design Patterns</li>\r\n  <li><strong>Behavioral Design Patterns</strong></li>\r\n  <li><strong>Structural Design Patterns</strong></li>\r\n  <li>Applicational Design Patterns</li>\r\n  <li><strong>Creational Design Patterns</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</article>\r\n";
// Exports
/* harmony default export */ __webpack_exports__["default"] = (code);

