var code = "<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <div id=\"link-to-quiz\" class=\"btn-back\">назад</div>\r\n\r\n  <h1>[St2] Event Loop & Animation</h1>\r\n\r\n  <strong>33 вопроса. Выполнил на 88 баллов из 100</strong>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n<h3>1. Based on the provided code execution diagram, what is the correct order of execution?\r\n</h3>\r\n<pre>setTimeout(() => alert(\"timeout\"));\r\n\r\nPromise.resolve().then(() => alert(\"promise\"));\r\n\r\nalert(\"code\");</pre>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>timeout, promise, code</li>\r\n  <li>code, timeout, promise</li>\r\n  <li>promise, code, timeout</li>\r\n  <li><strong>code, promise, timeout</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>2. Based on the provided code, what would the correct call stack sequence be?</h3>\r\n<pre>function multiply(a, b) {\r\n  return a * b;\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunction square(n) {\r\n  return multiply(n, n);\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunction printSquare() {\r\n  console.log(square(42));\r\n}\r\n\r\nprintSquare();</pre>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>printSquare, multiply, square</li>\r\n    <li><strong>printSquare, square, multiply</strong></li>\r\n    <li>square, multiply, printSquare</li>\r\n    <li>square, printSquare, multiply</li>\r\n    <li>printSquare</li>\r\n    <li>multiply, square, printSquare</li>\r\n    <li>multiply, printSquare, square</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>3. Bezier curves are used for creating: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>graphics on Canvas</strong></li>\r\n    <li>raster graphics</li>\r\n    <li><strong>SVG format</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>fonts</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>describing the path and speed of animation in CSS animation</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>vector graphics</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>4. For what purpose is the \"cubic-bezier()\" function used in CSS?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>To adjust an element's horizontal movement</li>\r\n    <li>To enlarge an element upon clicking</li>\r\n    <li><strong>To define a custom Bezier curve for animations</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>5. How does setting \"animation-direction: alternate-reverse\"; affect the playback of an animation?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>In reverse order, starting from the end</li>\r\n    <li>Randomly choosing the direction at the start of each cycle to increase unpredictability</li>\r\n    <li>Starting forward for the first iteration, then alternating direction with each cycle</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Starting in reverse for the first iteration, then alternating direction with each cycle</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Like a confused commuter deciding whether to go to work or home, then switching at every stop</li>\r\n    <li>In the original forward direction</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>6. How does the setInterval() method function in JavaScript?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Repeatedly calls a function or executes a code snippet, with a fixed time delay between each call</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Calls a function once after a specified time interval</li>\r\n    <li>Sets a delay for the entire program's execution</li>\r\n    <li>Executes a code snippet immediately, then at fixed intervals</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>7. How does the \"setTimeout(function timeout() {}, 5000);\" method function?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Multiple times, as defined by the second argument</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Once, after a specified period of time</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Immediately, without any delay</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>8. How is the refresh rate of a display quantified? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>fps</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>1/s</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Hz</strong></li>\r\n    <li>s</li>\r\n    <li><strong>s^(-1)</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>9. How many threads are there in JS?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Dependent on the number of cores in the CPU</li>\r\n    <li><strong>1</strong></li>\r\n    <li>As many as specified in the webpack configuration</li>\r\n    <li>2</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>10. Pick the default (initial) value of the timing function:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>easy-peasy</li>\r\n    <li>ease-out</li>\r\n    <li>ease-in</li>\r\n    <li><strong>ease</strong></li>\r\n    <li>ease-in-out</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>11. Select the keyframes snippet correctly representing the given leaf animation or both if they are equal. (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>(1)</strong>\r\n<pre>@keyframes leaf_anim {\r\n  0% {}\r\n  90% {}\r\n  100% {}\r\n}\r\n</pre>\r\n    </li>\r\n    <li><strong>(2)</strong>\r\n<pre>@keyframes leaf_anim {\r\n  from {}\r\n  90% {}\r\n  to {}\r\n}\r\n</pre>\r\n    </li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>12. The \"animation-fill-mode\" property:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>sets whether an animation running or paused</li>\r\n    <li>sets the number of times an animation sequence should be played before stopping</li>\r\n    <li><strong>sets how a CSS animation applies styles to its target before and after its execution</strong></li>\r\n    <li>specifies the amount of time to wait from applying the animation to an element before beginning to perform the animation</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>13. The animation property is a shorthand for: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>animation-timing-function</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>animation-duration</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>animation-delay</strong></li>\r\n    <li>animation-repeat</li>\r\n    <li><strong>animation-name</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>animation-iteration-count</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>animation-fill-mode</strong></li>\r\n    <li>animation-timestamp</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>14. The console output would be the following:</h3>\r\n<pre>function first() {\r\n  console.log(\"First function\");\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunction second() {\r\n  first();\r\n  console.log(\"Second function\");\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunction third() {\r\n  second();\r\n  console.log(\"Third function\");\r\n}\r\n\r\nthird();</pre>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>First function, Second function, Third function</strong></li>\r\n    <li>First function, Third function, Second function</li>\r\n    <li>Second function, First function, Third function</li>\r\n    <li>Third function, Second function, First function</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>15. The console output would be the following:</h3>\r\n<pre>let callback = () => console.log(\"just regular timeout callback has run\");\r\n\r\nlet urgentCallback = () => console.log(\"an urgent callback has run 😱\");\r\n\r\nconsole.log(\"main program started\");\r\n\r\nsetTimeout(callback, 0);\r\n\r\nqueueMicrotask(urgentCallback);\r\n\r\nconsole.log(\"main program exiting\");</pre>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>main program started, main program exiting, an urgent callback has run 😱, just regular timeout callback has run</strong></li>\r\n    <li>main program started, just regular timeout callback has run, an urgent callback has run 😱, main program exiting</li>\r\n    <li>main program started, an urgent callback has run 😱, just regular timeout callback has run, main program exiting</li>\r\n    <li>main program started, main program exiting, just regular timeout callback has run, an urgent callback has run 😱</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>16. The duration of an animation created with the .animate() method is measured in:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>light-years per snail pace</li>\r\n    <li><strong>milliseconds</strong></li>\r\n    <li>the method allows to specify either milliseconds or seconds</li>\r\n    <li>seconds</li>\r\n    <li>ticks</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>17. The ease-in-out built-in curve represents CSS animation that is:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>starts abruptly, but progressively slows down towards the end</li>\r\n    <li>begins slowly, increases in speed towards the end, and stops abruptly</li>\r\n    <li><strong>starts slowly, accelerates in the middle and and decelerates towards the end</strong></li>\r\n    <li>maintains a consistent speed throughout the animation</li>\r\n    <li>starts abruptly, slows down in the middle and finishes sharply</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>18. The minimum number of points to create the Bezier curve presented on the pic are:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>four points and the name of such curve is cubic</strong></li>\r\n    <li>two points and the name of such curve is parabolic</li>\r\n    <li>three points and the name of such curve is parabolic</li>\r\n    <li>four points and the name of such curve is parabolic</li>\r\n    <li>three points and the name of such curve is cubic</li>\r\n    <li>two points and the name of such curve is cubic</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>19. The possible steps of the rendering waterfall while animating CSS properties: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>reflow</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>composition</strong></li>\r\n    <li>rerender</li>\r\n    <li><strong>repaint</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>recalculate style</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>20. To apply the following ⚓ animation (assuming that the picture's element aspect-ratio property is 1 / 1) - the transform-origin property should be set to:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>center</li>\r\n    <li>bottom left</li>\r\n    <li>bottom right</li>\r\n    <li><strong>top center</strong></li>\r\n    <li>top left</li>\r\n    <li>top right</li>\r\n    <li>bottom center</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>21. What are the available rendering contexts for the HTML canvas element? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>CanvasRenderingContext3D</li>\r\n    <li><strong>CanvasRenderingContext2D</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>WebGLRenderingContext</strong></li>\r\n    <li>ImageBitmapRenderingContext</li>\r\n    <li>GPUCanvasContext</li>\r\n    <li>CPUCanvasContext</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>22. What are the correct descriptions of the code execution behavior for \"setTimeout(func, 0)\"? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Executes after processing calls from the call stack and the microtasks queue</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Calls \"func\" every second</li>\r\n    <li>Executes \"func\" first, irrespective of the tasks in the code</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Offers no 100% guarantee that func will be called exactly in 0 seconds</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>23. What is going to happen if we run this code?</h3>\r\n<pre>function foo() {\r\n  return foo();\r\n}\r\n\r\nfoo();</pre>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>The code executes successfully without causing the application to crash or throwing any errors</li>\r\n    <li>Stack overflow resulting in a ReferenceError exception</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Stack overflow resulting in a RangeError exception</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Stack overflow resulting in a TypeError exception</li>\r\n    <li>The code is a specific case that places the \"foo\" function into the macrotasks queue</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>24. What is the Call Stack in JavaScript?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>A location in a computer's RAM where functions are stored</li>\r\n    <li>Simply a designated space in browser memory</li>\r\n    <li><strong>A data structure and mechanism utilized by the JavaScript engine to manage function calls, record their positions within a program, and handle execution contexts</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>25. What is the execution order of microtasks and macrotasks?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>After executing each macrotask, the engine checks for any microtasks and executes them before moving on to the next macrotask, UI rerendering, or other operations</strong></li>\r\n    <li>The engine first executes all macrotasks, then proceeds to execute all microtasks</li>\r\n    <li>All tasks are executed sequentially, regardless of their type</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>26. What is the \"Heap\" in the context of JavaScript?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>A large, mostly unstructured, dynamically allocated region of memory</strong></li>\r\n    <li>An unordered version of the Call Stack</li>\r\n    <li>A principle governing the storage and execution of functions within the Call Stack</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>27. What is the most effective method to achieve smooth JavaScript animations?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>a custom user-defined animation function</li>\r\n    <li><strong>requestAnimationFrame()</strong></li>\r\n    <li>setInterval()</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>28. What principle governs the order of operations within the Call Stack?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>queue</li>\r\n    <li>Random</li>\r\n    <li>Priority Queue</li>\r\n    <li>FIFO</li>\r\n    <li><strong>LIFO</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>29. What will be the console output?</h3>\r\n<pre>setTimeout(() => {\r\n  console.log(\"First\");\r\n}, 1000);\r\n\r\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"Second\"));\r\n\r\nPromise.resolve(console.log(\"Third\"));\r\n\r\nPromise.resolve().then(() =>\r\n  setTimeout(() => {\r\n    console.log(\"Fourth\");\r\n  }, 0)\r\n);\r\n\r\nsetTimeout(() => {\r\n  console.log(\"Fifth\");\r\n}, 0);\r\n\r\nconsole.log(\"Last\");\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>First, Second, Third, Fourth, Fifth, Last</li>\r\n    <li>Last, Second, Third, Fourth, First, Fifth</li>\r\n    <li>Third, Last, Second, Fourth, First, Fifth</li>\r\n    <li>Third, Last, Second, First, Fourth, Fifth</li>\r\n    <li>Last, Second, Third, First, Fourth, Fifth</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Third, Last, Second, Fifth, Fourth, First</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>30. What will be the output of this code?</h3>\r\n<pre>console.log(\"start 🚗\");\r\n\r\nsetTimeout(function () {\r\n  console.log(\"🍾🏆\");\r\n}, 1000);\r\n\r\nsetTimeout(function () {\r\n  console.log(\"finish 🏁\");\r\n}, 100);\r\n\r\nPromise.resolve().then(function () {\r\n  console.log(\"pit-stop\");\r\n});\r\n\r\nconsole.log(\"let's go\");</pre>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>start 🚗, let's go, pit-stop, 🍾🏆, finish 🏁</li>\r\n    <li><strong>start 🚗, let's go, pit-stop, finish 🏁, 🍾🏆</strong></li>\r\n    <li>let's go, start 🚗, pit-stop, finish 🏁, 🍾🏆</li>\r\n    <li>pit-stop, let's go, start 🚗, finish 🏁, 🍾🏆</li>\r\n    <li>start 🚗, let's go,🍾🏆, finish 🏁, pit-stop</li>\r\n    <li>pit-stop, let's go, start 🚗, 🍾🏆, finish 🏁</li>\r\n    <li>start 🚗, 🍾🏆, finish 🏁, pit-stop, let's go</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>31. What will be the output of this code?</h3>\r\n<pre>console.log(\"hey\");\r\n\r\nsetTimeout(() => {\r\n  console.log(\"RSSchool crew\");\r\n}, 5000);\r\n\r\nconsole.log(\"there\");</pre>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>hey, there, RSSchool crew</strong></li>\r\n    <li>there, somewhere, lost in code, the RSSchool crew</li>\r\n    <li>there, hey, RSSchool crew</li>\r\n    <li>hey, RSSchool crew, there</li>\r\n    <li>there, RSSchool crew, hey</li>\r\n    <li>RSSchool crew, hey, there</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>32. Which CSS feature enables the gradual change of an element's properties over a specified period from initial to final state with no intermediate steps?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>CSS KeyFrames</li>\r\n    <li>CSS Transform</li>\r\n    <li><strong>CSS Transition</strong></li>\r\n    <li>CSS Animation</li>\r\n    <li>CSS Gradual Change</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>33. Which method is used to establish a drawing context on a canvas element?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>setContext()</li>\r\n    <li>defineContext()</li>\r\n    <li><strong>getContext()</strong></li>\r\n    <li>summonContext()</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>34. Which of the following are considered macrotasks in the JavaScript event loop? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Event handlers (addEventListener)</li>\r\n    <li>XMLHttpRequest</li>\r\n    <li>initiateSelfDestruct()</li>\r\n    <li>console.log()</li>\r\n    <li>fetch</li>\r\n    <li><strong>setTimeout()</strong></li>\r\n    <li>onload</li>\r\n    <li>queueMicrotask()</li>\r\n    <li><strong>setInterval()</strong></li>\r\n    <li>await expressions in async functions</li>\r\n    <li>mousemove</li>\r\n    <li>Promise</li>\r\n    <li><strong>I/O operations (file reading / writing)</strong></li>\r\n    <li>onclick</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>35. Which of the following events triggers when the CSS animation finishes?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>transitionend</li>\r\n    <li>onEnd</li>\r\n    <li><strong>animationend</strong></li>\r\n    <li>animationfinish</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>36. Which of the following is a widely used garbage collection algorithm?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>mark and delete</li>\r\n    <li><strong>mark and sweep</strong></li>\r\n    <li>mark and hide</li>\r\n    <li>mark and remove</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>37. Which of the following is considered a Microtask in JavaScript? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Promise</strong></li>\r\n    <li>synchronous code</li>\r\n    <li>setTimeout()</li>\r\n    <li><strong>queueMicrotask()</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>38. Which of the following statements accurately describe the Event Loop in JavaScript? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Creates a new context for a function called within another function, storing the previous context in the Call Stack</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Transfers tasks from the task queue to the call stack when the latter is empty</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Enables the execution of asynchronous code in JavaScript</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Its call stack has a maximum size determined by the browser or runtime environment</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Continuously checks the callback queue and the call stack</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Prioritizes execution of long-running operations to optimize performance.</li>\r\n    <li>Sends an SOS signal to external APIs when overloaded with callbacks, hoping for backup</li>\r\n    <li>Comprises macrotasks, synchronous code, and microtasks, each adhering to the principles of stack, queue, and list, respectively</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Automatically removes a function from the call stack upon its return or the end of its scope</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>39. Which properties are condensed into the shorthand \"transition\" CSS property? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>transition-direction</li>\r\n    <li><strong>transition-timing-function</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>transition-duration</strong></li>\r\n    <li>transition-time-property</li>\r\n    <li>transition-mood-swings</li>\r\n    <li><strong>transition-delay</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>40. Which types of layers are recognized in a web page's rendering process by Chrome? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>GraphicsLayer</strong></li>\r\n    <li>CSSLayer</li>\r\n    <li>ScrollLayer</li>\r\n    <li><strong>RenderLayer</strong></li>\r\n    <li>DOMLayer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>41. Why is it better to move an element with the translate() property rather than with the position: absolute? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>the animation looks smoother with more fluid motion</strong></li>\r\n    <li>the animation quality doesn't depend on CSS properties</li>\r\n    <li><strong>the animation speed increases</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>the browser better optimizes element animation rendering</strong></li>\r\n    <li>allows to make the position adjustments without affecting the layout of other elements</li>\r\n    <li>the translate() property calculates the coordinates of the animated element more precisely</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n";
// Exports
/* harmony default export */ __webpack_exports__["default"] = (code);

