__webpack_require__.r(__webpack_exports__);
// Module
var code = "<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <div id=\"link-to-quiz\" class=\"btn-back\">назад</div>\r\n\r\n  <h1>Test [St1] Modules in JavaScript</h1>\r\n\r\n  <strong>32 вопроса. Выполнил на 78 баллов из 100</strong>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>1. Are JavaScript modules compatible with older browsers lacking ES6 support?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Directly supported</li>\r\n    <li>Yes, using polyfills for backward compatibility</li>\r\n    <li>With server-side rendering</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Not compatible</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Only if you whisper sweet nothings into the browser's ear</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>2. Are module scripts always deferred?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Yes</strong></li>\r\n    <li>No</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>3. Can named exports in JavaScript modules be modified?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Yes, always</strong></li>\r\n    <li>No, named exports are immutable and read-only</li>\r\n    <li>Only in strict mode</li>\r\n    <li>Depends on the variable type</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>4. Can the 'import' keyword be used to import functionality from an index.html file?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Yes</li>\r\n    <li><strong>No</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>5. How can you rename an imported module in JavaScript?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Create new variable with new name in script which uses this import</li>\r\n    <li>Import the whole content of module and extract it with destructing syntax</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Use 'as' (e.g. import { getText as ExternalFunc } from './utils/')</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Imports can be imported and used only with their original names</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>6. How does JavaScript manage side effects within modules?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Ignored</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Executed once</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Not allowed</li>\r\n    <li>Depends on the export type</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>7. How does the inclusion of top-level 'await' affect the loading and execution of a script in an ES module?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Blocks module execution until the promise resolves</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Has no impact on script loading</li>\r\n    <li>Causes a syntax error</li>\r\n    <li>Delays loading of all dependent modules</li>\r\n    <li>It throws a dramatic pause into the script, like a cliffhanger in a soap opera</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>8. How many times does a JS module code imports?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>After every re-render</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Only once, at the initial import</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Varies depending on the code structure</li>\r\n    <li>None of above</li>\r\n    <li>Whenever it feels like it needs a change of scenery</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>9. How to enable modules in project?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Add attribute type=\"module\" to &lt;script&gt; tag</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Add to name of JS file identifier of module - script.module.js</li>\r\n    <li>Modules are enabled by default</li>\r\n    <li>None of the above</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>10. In a JavaScript ES6 module, what does the 'this' keyword refer to?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>global</li>\r\n    <li>window</li>\r\n    <li>null</li>\r\n    <li><strong>undefined</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>11. In JavaScript modules, how are live bindings different from typical variable or function references?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>They are constantly updated</strong></li>\r\n    <li>They are static references</li>\r\n    <li>No difference in behavior</li>\r\n    <li>Only applicable to functions</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>12. Is it possible for a JavaScript module to contain both a default export and named exports?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>No</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Yes</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Yes, by it should be one default and one named exports</li>\r\n    <li>Yes, but named exports should be maximum 2</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>13. Is it possible for JavaScript modules to export class declarations?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Yes, class declarations can be directly exported</strong></li>\r\n    <li>No, only instances</li>\r\n    <li>Only anonymous classes</li>\r\n    <li>Only if extended</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>14. Is it true that JavaScript modules only work with HTTP/HTTPS and require a local web server for local development?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Yes</strong></li>\r\n    <li>No</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>15. Is it true that module scripts execute before the HTML document is fully ready if the module's size is smaller than the HTML file?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Yes, they execute based on size comparison</li>\r\n    <li><strong>No, execution does not depend on file size</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>16. Is the 'export' keyword used to make variables and functions accessible outside their current JavaScript module?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Yes</strong></li>\r\n    <li>No</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>17. What are the key features of a JavaScript module? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Always work in strict mode</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Top-level variables and functions from a module are not seen in other scripts</strong></li>\r\n    <li>In a module, top-level's 'this' equals top-level's local scope</li>\r\n    <li><strong>A module code is evaluated only the first time when imported</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>18. What benefits do modules provide? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>This approach helps to divide code into reusable pieces of code</strong>This approach helps to divide code into reusable pieces of code</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Encapsulation of implementation details</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Easy dependencies management</li>\r\n    <li>Modules automatically download all dependecies</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>19. What correctly describes a JS module? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Module path must be a string and can’t be a function call</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Modules offer no benefits to developers</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Module can be imported conditionally or at run-time</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Modules can't load each other and interchange functionality</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>20. What does 're-export' mean in JavaScript modules?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Duplication of export in module code</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Ability to export within one module functionality imported into it from another module</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Ability of JS to export functionality again if something goes wrong</li>\r\n    <li>None of the above</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>21. What happens when a module imports itself in JavaScript?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Throws an error</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Generates a new instance of the module</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Ignored by the interpreter</li>\r\n    <li>Handled as a cyclic dependency</li>\r\n    <li>It gets caught in a narcissistic loop of self-admiration</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>22. What IIFE syntax is correct?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>(function() { ... })</li>\r\n    <li>function() { ... }</li>\r\n    <li><strong>(function() { ... })()</strong></li>\r\n    <li>All of the above</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>23. What information is contained in the 'meta' property of an 'import' object in JavaScript?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Information regarding the current module</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Settings or configurations of the module</li>\r\n    <li>List of imports</li>\r\n    <li>This property doesn't exist</li>\r\n    <li>Secret messages from other developers</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>24. What is a dynamic import?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Module import in conditional statement</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Function-like expression that allows loading modules asynchronously</li>\r\n    <li>Re-exported imports</li>\r\n    <li>All of the above</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>25. What is the difference between default and named export in a JS module? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Default and named exports are the same</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Only one default export is allowed per file, whereas multiple named exports are permitted</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Only one named export is accepted in one file, default exports can be multiple</li>\r\n    <li><strong>You can choose any name when importing default export, named exports are imported as it is or should be renamed explicitly</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>26. What is the maximum number of default exports allowed in a single JavaScript module file?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>0</li>\r\n    <li><strong>1</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Maximum 5</li>\r\n    <li>Unlimited number</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>27. What is the primary purpose of using an IIFE in JavaScript?</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Making code more readable</li>\r\n    <li>Better communication with server</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Reducing global scope pollution</strong></li>\r\n    <li>To create a secret society within your code</li>\r\n    <li>Improving of code quality</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>28. What types of JS modules exist? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>CommonJS</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Universal Module Definition (UMD)</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Asynchronous Module Definition (AMD)</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>ES6</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>29. Which of the following statements are correct about JS modules? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Module can be imported to another module</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Module is directory with scripts</li>\r\n    <li><strong>One script is one module</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Module usually includes 'export' statements in its code</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Modules are magical boxes where JS elves write your code</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>30. Which of the following statements correctly describe characteristics of IIFE functions in JavaScript? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Anonymous function that is invoked when it is declared</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Immediately Invoked Function Expression</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Initially Invoked Function Expression</li>\r\n    <li>Named function that is invoked when it is declared</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>31. Which statements about dynamic import are correct? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>It works in regular scripts and does not require the 'type=\"module\"' attribute in a &lt;script&gt; tag</strong></li>\r\n    <li>import() is regular function</li>\r\n    <li><strong>import() looks like function but it's special syntax</strong></li>\r\n    <li>'call' and 'apply' methods can be used with import()</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>32. Which ways to export code from JS module really exist? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>default export</strong></li>\r\n    <li>initial export</li>\r\n    <li>single export</li>\r\n    <li><strong>named export</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n";
// Exports
/* harmony default export */ __webpack_exports__["default"] = (code);

