var code = "<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <div id=\"link-to-quiz\" class=\"btn-back\">назад</div>\r\n\r\n  <h1>[St1] OOP Test</h1>\r\n\r\n  <strong>20 вопросов. Выполнил на 95 баллов из 100</strong>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>1. A collection of objects characterized by common methods and properties:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Object</li>\r\n    <li>Property</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Class</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Type</li>\r\n    <li>Method</li>\r\n    <li>The Methodical Mob</li>\r\n    <li>Method Collection</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>2. At what level of abstraction should one strive for when determining the number of classes?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>1-2 classes</li>\r\n    <li>2-3 classes</li>\r\n    <li>The more, the better</li>\r\n    <li><strong>The minimum necessary</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>3. Class diagrams in UML:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Are used to visualize and describe the dynamic aspects of a system or software application</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Are used to model the structure of object-oriented applications, including classes, their attributes, inheritance, class relationships, etc.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Are used to know how objects interact with each other during a certain period of time and visualizes the timing constraints of a system</li>\r\n    <li>Focus on lifelines, or the processes and objects that live simultaneously, the messages exchanged between them and the order of the interaction</li>\r\n    <li>Are essentially a flowchart that shows activities performed by a system</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>4. How is a destructor characterized in Object-Oriented Programming?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Called upon the creation of an object</li>\r\n    <li>Activated when any method within the class is called</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Automatically invoked when an object is destroyed</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Code's Retirement Ceremony</li>\r\n    <li>No correct answer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>5. How is the concept of \"composition\" understood in Object-Oriented Programming?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>One class contains and manages the lifecycle of an object from another class.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>A class agrees to fulfill a contract by defining all methods described in the interface</li>\r\n    <li>A class includes an object of another class without managing its lifecycle</li>\r\n    <li>An object of one type is connected to an object of another type</li>\r\n    <li>A class owns and is responsible for the objects it contains</li>\r\n    <li>No correct answer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>6. How is the concept of \"method overloading\" defined in Object-Oriented Programming?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>When 2 or more methods in a class have the same name but different parameters (varying in number or types)</strong></li>\r\n    <li>When the parameters in a class method have the same name</li>\r\n    <li>When 2 or more methods in a class have the same name but different parameters (different number of parameters, but not types)</li>\r\n    <li>No correct answer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>7. In Object-Oriented Programming, how is a constructor defined?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Called automatically when any method in the class is invoked</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Called automatically when an object of the class is created</strong></li>\r\n    <li>No correct answer</li>\r\n    <li>Called automatically when an object of the class is destroyed</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>8. In Object-Oriented Programming, what is used to describe and define the behavior of an object?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Fields</li>\r\n    <li>Objects</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Methods</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Classes</li>\r\n    <li>Behaviors</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>9. In the context of Object-Oriented Programming, how is the term \"association\" defined?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>A class fulfills a contract by defining all methods described within the interface</li>\r\n    <li><strong>An object of one type being somehow connected to an object of another type</strong></li>\r\n    <li>A relationship where one class contains an object of another class but does not manage the lifecycle of the other object</li>\r\n    <li>A relationship where one class contains an object of another class and manages the lifecycle of the other object</li>\r\n    <li>Friendship Between Code Classes</li>\r\n    <li>Objects work together to achieve common goals</li>\r\n    <li>No correct answer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>10. In the context of Object-Oriented Programming, what does \"implementation\" refer to?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>A class contains an object of another class and manages its lifecycle</li>\r\n    <li>An object of one type is somehow connected to an object of another type</li>\r\n    <li>A class contains an object of another class but does not manage the lifecycle of the other object</li>\r\n    <li><strong>A class agrees to fulfill a contract by defining all methods described in the interface</strong></li>\r\n    <li>No correct answer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>11. Interaction diagrams in UML:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Are used to know how objects interact with each other during a certain period of time and visualizes the timing constraints of a system</li>\r\n    <li>Focus on lifelines, or the processes and objects that live simultaneously, the messages exchanged between them and the order of the interaction</li>\r\n    <li>Are essentially a flowchart that shows activities performed by a system</li>\r\n    <li>Serve to model the structure of object-oriented applications, including classes, their attributes, inheritance, class relationships, etc.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Are used to visualize and describe the dynamic aspects of a system or software application</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>12. Is it possible for a single class in Object-Oriented Programming to implement multiple interfaces at the same time?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Yes, it can</strong></li>\r\n    <li>No, it cannot</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>13. Is it true that the 'protected' access modifier in Object-Oriented Programming allows accessibility from a derived class?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>True</li>\r\n    <li>False</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>14. Select the statements that are characteristic of an abstract class: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Contains common functionality for related objects</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Can create an instance</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Can contain fields</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Can contain unrealized methods that require implementation in child classes</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Can contain a constructor</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Can only contain public methods</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>15. Select the statements that are characteristic of an interface: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Can only contain public methods</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Can contain fields</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Can contain unrealized methods that require implementation in classes according to the contract</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Contains common functionality for related objects</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Can create an instance</li>\r\n    <li>Can contain a constructor</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>16. Select the subcategories of UML diagrams: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Structural</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Class</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Behavioral</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Object</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>17. Should classes describe all possible properties and actions of real-world objects?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>They should</strong></li>\r\n    <li>They should not</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>18. The main structural entities of the UML language are: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Collaborations</strong></li>\r\n    <li>State machines</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Use cases</strong></li>\r\n    <li>States</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Classes</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Components</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Interfaces</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>19. The property of objects where the same action with the same names results in different behavior for different objects:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Delegation</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Polymorphism</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Openness/Closure</li>\r\n    <li>Abstraction</li>\r\n    <li>Inheritance</li>\r\n    <li>Encapsulation</li>\r\n    <li>Interface Segregation</li>\r\n    <li>Dynamic Dispatch</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>20. The UML language was developed to: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Unify existing visual modeling methods</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Model the entire system using object-oriented methods</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Create a modeling language that can be used by humans and computers</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Address the scalability issue inherent in complex systems</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>21. Timing diagrams in UML:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Are used to visualize and describe the dynamic aspects of a system or software application</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Are used to know how objects interact with each other during a certain period of time and visualizes the timing constraints of a system</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Are essentially a flowchart that shows activities performed by a system</li>\r\n    <li>Focus on lifelines, or the processes and objects that live simultaneously, the messages exchanged between them and the order of the interaction</li>\r\n    <li>Serve to model the structure of object-oriented applications, including classes, their attributes, inheritance, class relationships, etc.</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>22. What are the distinctive features of the 'private' access modifier in Object-Oriented Programming? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Accessible only within the declaring class</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Accessible from a class that inherits from the declaring class</li>\r\n    <li>Accessible from any part of the program</li>\r\n    <li>The 'Keep Out' Sign of Programming</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>23. What is the defining characteristic of the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle in programming?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Avoiding unnecessary complexity in tasks</li>\r\n    <li>Not solving problems before they are required</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Minimizing code duplication for efficiency</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Optimizing code at an early development stage</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>24. What is the difference between an instance method and a static method?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>To call an instance method, it is necessary to create an instance of an object. A static method is not tied to an instance and can be called without creating an object</strong></li>\r\n    <li>To call a static method, it is necessary to create an instance of an object. An instance method can be called without creating an object</li>\r\n    <li>An instance method is a method inside a class. A static method is a method outside a class</li>\r\n    <li>There is no difference</li>\r\n    <li>A static method is a method inside a static class, and an instance method is a method inside an abstract class</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>25. What are the fundamental principles of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>Inheritance</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>Polymorphism</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Delegation</li>\r\n    <li>Openness/Closure</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Encapsulation</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Interface Segregation</li>\r\n    <li>Class Abstraction</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>26. What is the full meaning of the acronym \"OOP\" in programming?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Fundamentals of Object-Oriented Programming</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Object-Oriented Programming</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Object-Oriented Programs</li>\r\n    <li>Overly Optimistic Porcupine</li>\r\n    <li>Operational Object Programming</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>27. What is the term for an action or operation that an object can perform in Object-Oriented Programming?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Property</li>\r\n    <li>Event</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Method</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Digital Jugglery</li>\r\n    <li>Operation</li>\r\n    <li>No correct answer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n";
