var code = "<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <div id=\"link-to-quiz\" class=\"btn-back\">назад</div>\r\n\r\n  <h1>[St2] Web Security</h1>\r\n\r\n  <strong>33 вопроса. Выполнил на 79 баллов из 100</strong>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>1. A component that temporarily stores responses from the server so that they can then be reused for subsequent requests?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>HTTP/HTTPS protocol</li>\r\n    <li>CORS-mechanism</li>\r\n    <li>proxy server</li>\r\n    <li>HTTP cookies</li>\r\n    <li><strong>HTTP cache</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>2. A valid Content-Type header value for a \"simple\" CORS request is:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>text/plain</strong></li>\r\n    <li>gzip</li>\r\n    <li>html/form-data</li>\r\n    <li>multipart/plain</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n<h3>3. Choose correct statements:</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li><strong>HTTP uses port 80, HTTPS uses port 443</strong></li>\r\n  <li><strong>HTTPS uses encryption, which ensures the confidentiality of transmitted information.</strong></li>\r\n  <li>HTTPS is a protocol that is used only on sites that require storing customer data</li>\r\n  <li>According to the documentation, the data transfer speed of the HTTP protocol is slower than the HTTPS protocol</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n<h3>4. Differences between HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li><strong>HTTP/2 - binary</strong></li>\r\n  <li><strong>HTTP/2 has request prioritization as well as header compression</strong></li>\r\n  <li>HTTP/1.1 uses UDP instead of TCP as a transport protocol (unlike HTTP/2)</li>\r\n  <li>when using HTTP/1.1, the speed of sending requests increases sharply</li>\r\n  <li><strong>HTTP/2 uses one TCP connection, while HTTP/1.1 uses many</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>5. For which HTTP protocol versions do HTTP messages remain human-readable? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>HTTP/0.9</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>HTTP/1.1</strong></li>\r\n    <li>HTTP/2.0</li>\r\n    <li>HTTP/3.0</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>6. How does the Leaky Bucket algorithm differ from the Token Bucket?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>It allows bursts of traffic</strong></li>\r\n    <li>It adds tokens to a bucket</li>\r\n    <li>It removes tokens from the bucket at a fixed rate</li>\r\n    <li>It uses timestamps to track requests</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n<h3>7. How does the Same-origin policy help prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks?</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>By allowing scripts from any origin to access sensitive data</li>\r\n  <li><strong>By restricting scripts from one origin from accessing resources from another origin</strong></li>\r\n  <li>By encouraging developers to share scripts across multiple origins</li>\r\n  <li>All answers are right</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>8. How is the signature part of a JWT created?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>By encrypting the payload</li>\r\n    <li>By hashing the header</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Using a secret or a private key to sign the encoded header and payload</strong></li>\r\n    <li>By performing a ritual dance</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>9. HTTP protocol methods are: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>GET</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>PATCH</strong></li>\r\n    <li>SEND</li>\r\n    <li><strong>POST</strong></li>\r\n    <li>UPDATE</li>\r\n    <li><strong>DELETE</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>10. In the 'GET / HTTP/1.1' entry, the first slash indicates:</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>separator</li>\r\n    <li>absolute path on the site</li>\r\n    <li><strong>path to files on the server</strong></li>\r\n    <li>path to files on local machine</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>11. In JWT, what does the header typically consist of?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Payload data</li>\r\n    <li>Signature</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Token type and signing algorithm</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Encryption keys</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>12. Methods that allow you to asynchronously obtain network resources: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>get()</li>\r\n    <li>send()</li>\r\n    <li><strong>fetch()</strong></li>\r\n    <li>async()</li>\r\n    <li><strong>XMLHttpRequest()</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>13. Select existing HTTP header categories:(checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>representation headers</li>\r\n    <li>secondary headers</li>\r\n    <li><strong>request headers</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>response headers</strong></li>\r\n    <li>fetch metadata request headers</li>\r\n    <li>transfer process headers</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>14. Select HTTP request stages: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>client opens TCP connection</li>\r\n    <li><strong>client sends HTTP message to server</strong></li>\r\n    <li>server opens UDP connection</li>\r\n    <li><strong>the client reads the response sent by the server</strong></li>\r\n    <li>the client closes or reuses the connection to the server</li>\r\n    <li>server interrupts UDP connection</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>15. Select the correct statements regarding HTTP: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>unidirectional (request/response)</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>uses TCP as a transport layer</strong></li>\r\n    <li>can only transmit text data</li>\r\n    <li>only used in browsers</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>16. The response code 4xx indicates that... (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>HTTP request completed successfully</li>\r\n    <li><strong>There were problems with the request on the client side</strong></li>\r\n    <li>there were problems with processing the request on the server side`</li>\r\n    <li>the request was successfully received and processed</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>17. Valid methods for a \"simple\" CORS request: (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>GET</strong></li>\r\n    <li>PUT</li>\r\n    <li>DELETE</li>\r\n    <li><strong>HEAD</strong></li>\r\n    <li>PATCH</li>\r\n    <li><strong>POST</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>18. What can be contained in an HTTP request? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>HTTP method (name)</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>path to resource</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>HTTP protocol version</strong></li>\r\n    <li><strong>headers</strong></li>\r\n    <li>responses</li>\r\n    <li>body</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>19. What data format does a POST request sent with fetch() support?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>JSX</li>\r\n    <li>webp</li>\r\n    <li>TSX</li>\r\n    <li><strong>JSON</strong></li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>20. What is a downside of the Fixed Window Counter algorithm?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>It is complex to implement</li>\r\n    <li>It can allow bursts of requests at the window boundary</li>\r\n    <li><strong>It is not memory efficient</strong></li>\r\n    <li>It does not support distributed systems</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>21. Which is a server application that acts as an intermediary between a client requesting a resource and the server providing that resource?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>HTTP/HTTPS protocol</li>\r\n    <li>CORS-mechanism</li>\r\n    <li><strong>proxy server</strong></li>\r\n    <li>HTTP cookies</li>\r\n    <li>HTTP cache</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>22. What is cookie?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>open standard for creating access tokens based on JSON format</li>\r\n    <li>web application software methods and protocols used to store data in a web browser</li>\r\n    <li><strong>a small piece of data sent by a web server and stored on the user's computer</strong></li>\r\n    <li>API for client storage of large volumes of structured data</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>23. What is HTTP?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>a special protocol that is responsible for storing DNS addresses of sites</li>\r\n    <li><strong>network data protocol</strong></li>\r\n    <li>application protocol for storing and transmitting data between servers</li>\r\n    <li>encrypted data transmission protocol which provides enhanced security</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>24. What is MIME Type?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>standard describing the format of a document, file, or set of bytes</strong></li>\r\n    <li>HTTP protocol extension which supports encryption for improved security</li>\r\n    <li>an extension to the widely used FTP data transfer protocol that adds support for cryptographic protocols</li>\r\n    <li>extensible markup language</li>\r\n    <li>an open standard multimedia container that is the primary file and streaming format`</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>25. What is phishing?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Phishing is exploiting browser vulnerabilities. Users can prevent it by updating browsers and installing patches.</li>\r\n    <li>Phishing is intercepting data to steal personal information. Users can prevent it by encrypting connections and avoiding public Wi-Fi.</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Phishing is impersonating entities to steal sensitive information. Users can prevent it by checking email sender addresses, avoiding suspicious links, and enabling two-factor authentication.</strong></li>\r\n    <li>All of them</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>26. What is the primary purpose of JSON Web Tokens (JWT)?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>To securely transmit information between parties as a JSON object</strong></li>\r\n    <li>To encrypt data sent over the internet</li>\r\n    <li>To increase web page loading speed</li>\r\n    <li>To store data in the local storage</li>\r\n    <li>To bake cookies</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>27. What is the primary purpose of rate limiting?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>To enhance the UI/UX design</li>\r\n    <li><strong>To limit the number of requests to an endpoint</strong></li>\r\n    <li>To increase the response time of an API</li>\r\n    <li>To improve SEO rankings</li>\r\n    <li>To make developers' lives harder</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>28. What is the primary purpose of the HTTP Authorization header?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>To provide authentication credentials for HTTP requests</strong></li>\r\n    <li>To encrypt data sent over HTTP</li>\r\n    <li>To redirect users to a login page</li>\r\n    <li>To store cookies in the browser</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>29. What is the purpose of the Same-origin policy in web security?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>To ensure that all websites look similar</li>\r\n    <li><strong>To restrict how web pages or scripts from one origin can interact with resources from another origin</strong></li>\r\n    <li>To speed up web browsing by allowing unlimited cross-origin access</li>\r\n    <li>To make one deploy both for back and front</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>30. What is used to protect resources from potentially harmful documents and reduce attack vectors?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>CORS</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Same-origin Policy</strong></li>\r\n    <li>JSONP</li>\r\n    <li>JSONC</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>31. What layer of the OSI model does HTTP belong to?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>to application layer</strong></li>\r\n    <li>to the presentation layer</li>\r\n    <li>to session layer</li>\r\n    <li>to transport layer</li>\r\n    <li>to the network layer</li>\r\n    <li>to data link layer</li>\r\n    <li>to physical layer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>32. What layer of the OSI model does TCP belong to?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>to application layer</li>\r\n    <li>to the presentation layer</li>\r\n    <li>to session layer</li>\r\n    <li><strong>to transport layer</strong></li>\r\n    <li>to the network layer</li>\r\n    <li>to data link layer</li>\r\n    <li>to physical layer</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>33. What should not be stored in a JWT to avoid security issues?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>The user's favorite color</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Sensitive session data</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Public information</li>\r\n    <li>Non-essential data</li>\r\n    <li>Alien secrets</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>34. What the HTTP Response Code Shows?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>number of seconds the server responds to a request</li>\r\n    <li><strong>whether the HTTP request was completed successfully, status</strong></li>\r\n    <li>encryption used by the server</li>\r\n    <li>response codes are only provided by HTTPS protocol</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>35. What type of encryption does HTTPS use?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>SSH</li>\r\n    <li><strong>TLS</strong></li>\r\n    <li>SET</li>\r\n    <li>SSO</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>36. What type of encryption is used to encrypt data in TLS?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li><strong>symmetrical</strong></li>\r\n    <li>asymmetrical</li>\r\n    <li>depends on the server</li>\r\n    <li>no encryption used</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>37. What type of encryption is used to encrypt the key in TLS?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>no encryption used</li>\r\n    <li>depends on the server</li>\r\n    <li><strong>asymmetrical</strong></li>\r\n    <li>symmetrical</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>38. What type of token is typically used with the Bearer authentication scheme?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>SSL Certificate</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Access token</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Refresh token</li>\r\n    <li>API key</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>39. What types of HTTP messages are there?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>responses</li>\r\n    <li>requests</li>\r\n    <li><strong>requests and responses</strong></li>\r\n    <li>headers and responses</li>\r\n    <li>headers and requests</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>40. Which are correct statements about MFA? (checkbox)</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Requires multiple forms of authentication</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Enhances security against unauthorized access</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Encrypts data stored on a web server</li>\r\n    <li>None of them</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>41. Which front-end library for HTTP requests is based on promises?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Fetch API</li>\r\n    <li><strong>axios</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Got</li>\r\n    <li>curl</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>42. Which HTTP method is used for preflight CORS request?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>GET</li>\r\n    <li>PUT</li>\r\n    <li><strong>OPTIONS</strong></li>\r\n    <li>HEAD</li>\r\n    <li>PATCH</li>\r\n    <li>POST</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>43. Which is a server application that acts as an intermediary between a client requesting a resource and the server providing that resource?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>HTTP/HTTPS protocol</li>\r\n    <li>CORS-mechanism</li>\r\n    <li><strong>proxy server</strong></li>\r\n    <li>HTTP cookies</li>\r\n    <li>HTTP cache</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>44. Which mechanism uses additional headers to access resources on a domain other than what the site is currently using?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>xml</li>\r\n    <li>XMLHttpRequest</li>\r\n    <li><strong>CORS</strong></li>\r\n    <li>MIME</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>45. Which type of rate limiter cannot be bypassed by using multiple clients?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Client-side rate limiter</li>\r\n    <li><strong>Server-side rate limiter</strong></li>\r\n    <li>Middleware rate limiter</li>\r\n    <li>Database rate limiter</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n\r\n\r\n<article class=\"article\">\r\n  <h3>46. Which version of the HTTP protocol is still relevant today, despite being adopted in 1999?</h3>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>2.0</li>\r\n    <li>0.9</li>\r\n    <li><strong>1.1</strong></li>\r\n    <li>3.0</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</article>\r\n";
// Exports
/* harmony default export */ __webpack_exports__["default"] = (code);

